<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Vaduz</h1><p class="st-meta">1342 CE – 1719 CE · capital Vaduz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Alpine Rhine-valley county that, joined with Schellenberg in 1719, became the Principality of Liechtenstein.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The county was created in 1342 when the counts of Werdenberg-Sargans divided their lands and Hartmann III took the territory around Vaduz on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. In 1396 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iv-of-bohemia">King Wenceslaus</a> confirmed it as a direct fief of the empire, answerable only to the emperor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Vaduz was a rural county of villages and alpine pastures, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaduz-castle">Vaduz Castle</a> by its counts and their bailiffs. It passed to the barons of Brandis in 1416, to the counts of Sulz in 1510 and to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-hohenems">County of Hohenems</a> in 1613. Witch trials under the Hohenems counts in the mid and late 17th century were so severe that the emperor intervened and removed the count in 1684.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The bankrupt <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-hohenems">Hohenems</a> counts sold Vaduz in 1712 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-adam-i-prince-of-liechtenstein">Prince Johann Adam Andreas of Liechtenstein</a>, who had already bought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-schellenberg">Lordship of Schellenberg</a>. In 1719 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Charles VI</a> united the two as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein</a>, of which Vaduz is the capital today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-vaduz.jpg" alt="County of Vaduz" width="320" height="520"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oberland_in_Liechtenstein.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1342 CE – 1719 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vaduz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hartmann III of Werdenberg-Sargans</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vaduz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1530&amp;focus=county-of-vaduz" aria-label="County of Vaduz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4903 523 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Vaduz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5223 779L5226 772M5223 779L5223 779"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5226 772h0M5223 779h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5223 779h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5223 779h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vaduz. <a href="/atlas?year=1530&amp;focus=county-of-vaduz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
